Tournament Analysis
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  vertices: exchange audit patterns
  observable: bad lowerings, below-floor rows, no-cheap residual, full covers
  switch: harder = more bad lowerings, then below-floor, then no-cheap
  fingerprints: {'score_hist': {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1}, 'directed_3_cycles': 0, 'sccs': [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1], 'hardness_order': ['one_unit_lift_all_slack', 'Vstar_slack', 'first_open_gap_slack', 'AP_slack', 'zero_slack_control', 'double3_control']}

Synthesis
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bounded evidence supports the exchange lemma in a sharper form:
  after HYP-2095 removes rows with an unblocked small pair, the audited
  full D/U/N unit-lift residual is empty.
In the one-lift exhaustive scan, every full cover already has a cheap
unblocked-pair witness.  In the named local/extreme unit stress,
AP/V*/open-gap families again have no bad lowerings or below-floor
residuals.
The two non-cover controls show why D9/D12-style composite debt cannot be
repaired by unit representatives: no full-cover residual survives there.
Next proof move: formalize this as a local exchange rule: a lifted unit
representative that is not part of an unblocked small-pair witness can be
lowered shellwise, because unit representatives do not repair the gcd-3
D-obligations that make the canonical slack layer fail.
